{
  "outcome": "needs_user_info",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi Leo, sorry this has taken more than one request. I've found the problem: your laptop is almost out of storage. Only about 9 GB of 256 GB is free (3.5%), and when the drive gets that full, Windows can't save files. Here's how to free up space, quickest wins first:\n\n1. Empty the Recycle Bin, then delete anything you don't need from your Downloads folder.\n2. Go to Settings > System > Storage > Temporary files. Tick the temporary files and old Windows Update files and click Remove.\n3. Turn on OneDrive Files On-Demand: right-click the OneDrive cloud icon near the clock, then Settings > Sync and back up > Advanced settings > Files On-Demand. Choose 'Free up disk space' so your synced files stay in the cloud until you open them.\n4. Uninstall apps you no longer use (Settings > Apps > Installed apps).\n\nWhen you're done, reply to this ticket and I'll check your free space from our side. If it's still too low after the cleanup, I'll send it straight to our Tier 2 team to look at a bigger drive or a replacement laptop.",
  "internal_note": "User: Leo Martins (user_id 6), device BL-LT-0106. The user says this is their third request. Device check: online, Windows 11 Pro, 256 GB disk, 9.0 GB free (3.5%), active low disk space warning. This matches KB-017 (files won't save when free space is under 10%). I sent the user the KB-017 user-side cleanup steps: Recycle Bin/Downloads, temp files and old Windows Update files, OneDrive Files On-Demand, uninstalling unused apps. Next step: when the user confirms cleanup is done, re-run check_device_status. If free space is still under 10% (about 25.6 GB), escalate to Tier 2 for a larger drive or replacement per KB-017. Because this is a repeat request, please prioritise the follow-up. If the user can't do the cleanup themselves, consider a remote session.",
  "kb_articles": [
    "KB-017"
  ],
  "confidence": "high"
}
